Nethack 3.0.10 is an admirable piece of work. Some quite clever ideas have
been incorporated into the game by some talented hackers.

However...

Some of Nethack's ideas have not been fully developed, and they often
lack consistency. Here are a few suggestions for improvements:

Intrinsics:

An intrinsic range of 1-99 makes a lot more sense than AD&D's confusing
3-18-18/** scheme. 

Character classes: 

I'm not crazy about the default alignments set for various characters.
Perhaps let players choose their initial alignments when they start,
with perhaps some constraints by character class (i.e. no lawful rogues,
no chaotic archaeologists).

Plus, all characters should start off with at least one food ration, to
prevent early starvation.

Why do barbarians have poison resistance? Barbarians are good fighters, 
admittedly, but shouldn't have any intrinsic capabilities short of their
ability to recognize weapons.

Cavemen shouldn't have bows and arrows; they're supposed to be primitive,
neanderthal types, right? Leather armor is fine, a bunch of rocks for
throwing, a club, plus lots of strength and constitution (perhaps more
than they get currently).

I'm fairly happy with the healer class. However, healers should get
some sort of lab coat/cloak as armor. Plus a carrot or two instead of
some of those apples might be a nice touch. 

Knights are ok, except their dexterity and strength are set a little low
at startup.

Priests need some work. First of all, I don't see a good reason why priests
should start off with such fine armor and weaponry. Forget the mace, chain mail
and shield. Give a priest a cloak or robe of some sort (cassock?) and a
staff as weapon. Perhaps a hat too (turban?). His spellbooks should be blessed,
but should be chosen from a "priestly" selection of spells (eg. clairvoyance,
cause fear, confuse monster, create monster, detect food, detect unseen,
healing, restore ability, turn undead). Priests should get warning ability
earlier than everyone else. They shouldn't have any better kicking ability
than any other class.

Rogues are good. Give them a blessed spellbook of detect treasure on 
startup, though. 

Valkyries are good, except I don't think they should start with stealth.
Give them stealth at level 10 or 15. 

Wizards are not bad. But give them a +0 rather than a +1 athame, and
make sure every wizard starts out with a magic marker and a couple of
blank scrolls. And try to avoid giving wizards initial items of dubious
usefulness, like wands of nothing, potions of hallucination, etc. 
Further, wizards should be able to detect magic. This would allow them
to be able to distinguish between enchanted (+ or -) armor and weapons
and unenchanted ones (but not know the actual enchantment value), and between
magic items (magic flute/horn/whistle/bag/lamp) and their mundane equivalents. 

There's room for a few more character types in Nethack. The alchemist idea
recently seen on rec.games.hack was interesting. A corresponding class
with expertise in scrolls/spellbooks might be an "archivist" class. A 
dwarf class is an obvious possible addition:

Dwarf: starts off with standard dwarven armor, pickaxe or 2-hand sword. 
Identifies gems like archaeologist. Strong, clumsy, sturdy, stupid, wise, and
ugly. Sleep resistant, fast at level 7, poison resistant at 20.

Make dwarves "@" instead of "h".

Fountains:

Their tendancy to dry up at the slightest provocation doesn't make much
sense. Apart from that, their functionality is good.

Sinks:

Sinks shouldn't turn into fountains by mere drinking. Kicking it, on the other
hand, should often result in this.

Thrones:

Every throne should have a king (or queen) of some sort of race sitting
on it. The attendees in the throne room should be of the same race as the
king sitting on the throne. 

Altars:

There should be a temple level (like the castle level) with temples for
all three alignments. That'll guarantee an altar for every alignment. Unicorns
of all three alignments should exist on this level.

Players should be able to sacrifice artifacts at altars.

Pets:

Innate "aggravate monster" isn't a very nice (or realistic) attribute to
gain when eating your pet. It's probably better to lose lots of luck instead.

Praying and deities:

Deities should be more responsive to your immediate problems when praying,
esp. low hit points.

#pray should offer a secondary menu, something like:
 Petition/Repent/Worship [P|R|W]?
Petition should behave like the current #pray. Repent should reconcile
an angry god (but the god might impose a penance, like an iron ball, 
reduced hit points, or reduced luck). Repent should do nothing if the
god isn't angry. Worship should do little or nothing, except at an altar, 
where it might cure hit points. Worshipping at an altar of another god
should anger one's own god, as should behaviour out of alignment. 

If a player petitions before the time-out period, a more informative message
should be given than (eg.) "Crom gets angry." How about "Crom gets annoyed
at your frequent whining"?

Monsters:

Nethack's variety of monsters is one of its greatest strengths. Some minor
suggestions:

Very difficult monsters should carry useful items and treasure. Dragons should
carry lots of gold/gems, for example.

Lichs, as former mages of great power, should carry a significant collection
of wands, scrolls and potions. This'll encourage players to avoid genociding
lichs. 

Elves should carry lembas wafers. 

Killer bees should be unaggressive until one bee is attacked or the hive is
entered. They're too often the death of promising young characters.
Furthermore, the hives should be full of honeycombs as well as royal jelly.

Give Medusa a significant item (artifact) to carry. Characters need to be
rewarded for killing her.

Trolls should never be regenerated with items when being resurrected.

Eating chameleons should give intrinsic polymorph, polymorph control, like
eating tengus gives intrinsic teleportation, teleport control.

The Quantum mechanic Erwin (Schrodinger) should carry an enormous box, 
containing a cat, which may or may not be dead. :-)

Shops:

Storekeepers should get angry when their store wall is breached. Dwarves,
rock moles, and other digging monsters should not penetrate store walls.
That'll make robbing stores quite difficult. Also, shopkeepers should get
angry automatically if you start digging holes in their shop floor.

However, shopkeepers should allow someone to enter with a pickaxe.

The killing of a shopkeeper or other peaceful human should not remove ESP 
capability (why should it?) Perhaps it should reduce your luck, though.

Shopkeepers shouldn't close for inventory. What, they don't want business?

Walking in and out of stores shouldn't identify mimics.

Engraving:

Athames should become dull from engraving like everything else. Why give
wizards such a big advantage? If they're given magic markers as suggested
above, they still can take advantage of Elbereth.

Intrinsics:

The polymorph and polymorph control intrinsic should be attainable by
eating some monster (mimic for polymorph, chameleon for poly control?)

You should be capable of gaining a "resist stoning" intrinsic, perhaps from
eating a yellow dragon or wearing yellow dragon scale mail. 

Spells:

Spells should not wear out. Why should frequent use cause a spell to be
forgotten? 

To keep spells from becoming too powerful, make their cost in energy points
much, much higher. 

Corpses:

Why should lizard corpses not spoil? They should spoil like everything else.
Instead, let acid-based corpses (acid blob, yellow dragon, etc) not spoil.

Blessed corpses should not spoil at all. Currently, blessed corpses remain
edible for as long as they last, but they still disappear like other corpses.

Nurses and elves (dwarves?) are people too! Eating their corpses should be
cannibalism.

Eating:

You shouldn't choke when eating too much. Instead, if a character eats too
much, he should vomit profusely, and be placed in a fainting state. Or be
slowed for a significant length of time. Or both. 

Spoiled corpses should smell bad. Eg:
  This smells spoiled. Eat it? [y|n]
Or perhaps add a "sniff" command. 

Amulets:

The "amulet of change" should be "amulet of polymorph" and should give
the player a "polymorph" intrinsic while worn. Make it cursed, usually. 

Armor:

Water walking boots should give some indication of their nature if you
walk over a fountain. 

Gauntlets of power should work like gauntlets of dexterity: add their
enchantment level to your strength.

Gems: 

All humans who accept money (Shopkeepers, Oracle, Temple Priests) should
accept gems in lieu of money, perhaps with some discount applied.

Potions:

A blessed potion of blindness should ask for a number of turns of blindness,
much like a blessed potion of levitation. 

A blessed potion of booze should cure hallucination, confusion ("hair of the
dog that bit you", anyone?).

A blessed potion of hallucination should have little or no effect.

A blessed potion of paralysis should have little or no effect. 

A cursed potion of restore ability should decrease some (random) stat.

A blessed potion of speed should be (optionally) permanent. A cursed potion
of speed should slow you down.

If a vapour effect occurs, the player should have a chance to #name the
potion causing it.

Scrolls:

A cursed scroll of charging should reduce charges to 0 (or even -1). 

A blessed scroll of create monster should give a choice of monster to create.

A blessed scroll of gold detection should also detect gems.

How about a scroll of stair detection?

Items:

The interface for bags, chests, should be improved. You shouldn't need
to go through all items sequentially to add/remove them from a bag.

Wands:

Cursed wands should have a probability of failing when zapped. 

Wands of polymorph shouldn't polymorph more than 20ish items. 

Weapons:

Crysknives should not revert to worm teeth when unwielded if they're blessed.
Furthermore, they should permit engraving without being dulled. That'll give
people the incentive to enchant their worm teeth. 

Artifacts:

If an artifact is of a different alignment than the player, it should not be
usable. Some artifacts should not have alignments, though. 

Some artifacts should only be found in the hands of fearsome opponents.

I welcome any comments.

John
